{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":70970708,"ns":0,"title":"HD 125248","revisions":[{"revid":1378959744,"parentid":1330645934,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-10-07T02:58:53Z","size":19344,"sha1":"7ee98c9dffe44567bc2476ec885a894f4e147a9b","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|Binary star system in the constellation of Virgo}}\n{{Starbox begin}}\n{{Starbox image\n | image=\n{{Location mark\n|image=Libra constellation map.svg|alt=|float=center|width=240\n|label=|position=right\n|mark=Red circle.svg|mark_width=10|mark_link=HD 125248\n|x=890|y=504\n}}\n|caption=Location of HD 125248 (circled)\n}}\n{{Starbox observe\n | epoch       = [[J2000.0]]\n | constell    = [[Virgo (constellation)|Virgo]]\n | ra          = {{RA|14|18|38.251}}<ref name=GaiaEDR3/>\n | dec         = {{DEC|−18|42|57.47}}<ref name=GaiaEDR3/>\n | appmag_v    = {{val|5.84|to|5.95}}<ref name=Samus_et_al_2017/>\n}}\n{{Starbox character\n | type        = [[Main sequence]]<ref name=ozuyar2018/>\n | class       = A1p&nbsp;SrCrEu<ref name=Renson_Manfroid_2009/> or ApSi(Cr)<ref name=Houk_Smith-Moore_1978/>\n | b-v         = 0.013{{±|0.006}}<ref name=Anderson_Francis_2012/>\n | variable    = [[Alpha2 Canum Venaticorum variable|α<sup>2</sup> CVn]]<ref name=Samus_et_al_2017/>\n}}\n{{Starbox astrometry\n | radial_v    = −8.0{{±|0.8}}<ref name=Gontcharov2006/>\n | prop_mo_ra  = −52.197\n | prop_mo_dec = −46.004\n | pm_footnote = <ref name=GaiaEDR3/>\n | parallax    = 11.497\n | p_error     = 0.5361\n | parallax_footnote = <ref name=GaiaEDR3/>\n | absmag_v    = 0.83<ref name=Anderson_Francis_2012/>\n}}\n{{Starbox orbit\n | reference   = <ref name=Hockey1969/>\n | period      = 4.4\n | period_unitless = <!--(P) Period (no units provided by template)-->\n | axis        = <!--(a) Semimajor axis (in arcseconds)-->\n | axis_unitless = ≥{{cvt|161|Gm|AU}}\n | eccentricity = 0.21{{±|0.01}}\n | inclination = <!--(i) Inclination (in degrees)-->\n | node        = <!--(Ω) Longitude of node (in degrees)-->\n | periastron  = {{val|2431475.0|ul=JD|fmt=commas}}\n | periarg     = 82.4{{±|3.4}}\n | periarg_primary = <!--(ω) Argument of periastron (in degrees), primary -->\n | k1          = 7.5{{±|0.5}}\n | k2          = <!-- Velocity semi-amplitude (secondary in SB2), in km/s -->\n}}\n{{Starbox detail\n | mass        = 2.0{{±|0.6}}<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/>\n | radius      = 1.95<ref name=Shulyak_et_al_2014/>\n | luminosity  = 42.0{{±|8.3}}<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/>\n | temperature = {{val|9494|143}}<ref name=Borisov2023/>\n | gravity     = {{val|3.94|0.09}}<ref name=Borisov2023/>\n | metal_fe    = {{val|0.96|0.16}}<ref name=Borisov2023/>\n | rotational_velocity = 18<ref name=Royer_et_al_2002/>\n | rotation    = {{val|9.29558|0.00006|u=days|fmt=commas}}<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/>\n | age_myr     = 247<ref name=Borisov2023/>\n}}\n{{Starbox catalog\n | names = {{odlist | V=CS Vir | BD=−18°3789 | FK5=1369 | GC=19295 | HD=125248 | HIP=69929 | HR=5355 | PPM=228604 | SAO=158481 | Gould=236 G. Virginis }}<ref name=SIMBAD/>\n}}\n{{Starbox reference\n | Simbad = CS+Vir\n}}\n{{starbox end}}\n\n'''HD 125248''' is a [[binary star]]<ref name=Eggleton_Tokovinin_2008/> system in the equatorial [[constellation]] of [[Virgo (constellation)|Virgo]]. It has the [[variable star designation]] '''CS Virginis''', while ''HD 125248'' is the designation from the ''[[Henry Draper Catalogue]]''. This system is dimly visible to the [[naked eye]] as a point of light with an [[apparent visual magnitude]] that ranges from 5.84 down to 5.95.<ref name=Samus_et_al_2017/> It is located at a distance of approximately 280&nbsp;[[light years]] from the [[Sun]] based on [[stellar parallax|parallax]] measurements,<ref name=GaiaEDR3/> but is drifting closer with a heliocentric [[radial velocity]] of −8&nbsp;km/s.<ref name=Gontcharov2006/>\n\nThis star was classified as [[Chemically peculiar star|peculiar]] with [[spectral type]] A0p in the ''Henry Draper Catalogue'', published  1918–1924. This class was based on the strength of a pair of [[absorption line|lines]] of ionized silicon in its [[stellar spectrum]]. In 1931, [[William Wilson Morgan|W. W. Morgan]] discovered that the spectrum of the star varied over a period of several days. In particular, the lines of ionized chromium and europium varied considerably in intensity, ranging from strong to very faint. This variation shares similarities to those of [[Alpha2 Canum Venaticorum|α<sup>2</sup> Canum Venaticorum]]. The two sets of lines vary in the opposite direction from each other, so that the chromium lines were minimum when the europium lines were maximized, and vice versa.<ref name=Morgan1931/> In 1947, [[Armin Joseph Deutsch|A. J. Deutsch]] found a period of 9.295 days for the variation.<ref name=Deutsch1947/>\n\n[[Horace W. Babcock|H. W. Babcock]] examined the star using [[Coudé spectrogram]]s in 1947, finding a general [[stellar magnetic field|magnetic field]] with a strength of around {{val|5500|ul=Gauss|fmt=commas}} at the poles. At the time, that was the strongest magnetic field that had been observed in a star. He noticed that the magnetic field was variable, and it showed the opposite [[Magnetic polarity|polarity]] when the lines of europium were at a minimum compared to when they were at the maximum.<ref name=Babcock1947/> Subsequent observations showed the period and amplitude of the variation to be stable over time.<ref name=Hockey1969/> In 1950, [[Walter Stibbs|D. W. N. Stibbs]] first proposed an 'oblique rotator model' to explain the properties of this star,<ref name=Stibbs1950/> in which its magnetic field is locked at an angle to the [[axis of rotation]].<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/>\n\n[[File:CSVirLightCurve.png|thumb|left|A [[light curve]] for CS Virginis, plotted from ''[[Hipparcos]]'' data<ref name=HIPCurve/>]]\nThe star displays [[radial velocity]] variations that suggest it is a single-lined [[spectroscopic binary]] with a period of 4.4&nbsp;years and an [[orbital eccentricity]] of 0.21.<ref name=Hockey1969/> The visible component has a [[stellar classification]] of A1p&nbsp;SrCrEu,<ref name=Renson_Manfroid_2009/> which indicates this is a magnetic peculiar [[Ap star]] with prominent abundance anomalies of strontium, chromium, and europium in its [[stellar atmosphere|atmosphere]]. It has double the mass and nearly twice the radius of the Sun.<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/><ref name=Shulyak_et_al_2014/> The star is an estimated 234&nbsp;million years old and is spinning with a rotation rate of 9.3&nbsp;days.<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/> It is radiating 42 times the luminosity of the Sun from its [[photosphere]] at an [[effective temperature]] of 9,850&nbsp;K.<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/>\n\nIn 1950, Douglas Walter Noble Stibbs published data showing that HD 125248's brightness [[variable star|varies]].<ref name=\"Nature1950\"/> It is an [[Alpha2 Canum Venaticorum variable|Alpha<sup>2</sup> Canum Venaticorum variable]] star with a brightness that is modulated by its rotation.<ref name=Samus_et_al_2017/> Magnetic [[Doppler imaging]] of the star suggests the magnetic field deviates strongly from a simple [[dipole]] geometry.<ref name=Rusomarov_et_al_2016/>\n{{clear left}}\n\n==References==\n<references>\n<ref name=SIMBAD>{{cite simbad\n | title=CS Vir | access-date=2022-06-06 | postscript=. | mode=cs2 }}</ref>\n\n<ref name=GaiaEDR3>{{Cite Gaia EDR3|6284165975916129280}}</ref>\n\n<ref name=Houk_Smith-Moore_1978>{{citation\n | last1=Houk | first1=Nancy | last2=Smith-Moore | first2=M.\n | title=Michigan catalogue of two-dimensional spectral types for the HD stars\n | volume=4 | publication-place=Ann Arbor | year=1978\n | publisher=Dept. of Astronomy, University of Michigan\n | bibcode=1988mcts.book.....H | postscript=. }}</ref>\n\n<ref name=HIPCurve>{{citation\n | title=Light Curve | postscript=.\n | url=https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/hipparcos/java-tools/light-curve\n | website=Hippa